{"name":"Sylph of Moonlight","hex":"#524efb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sylph","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An invisible being of the air.","The elemental being of air, usually female."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sylph"},{"term":"moonlight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The light reflected from the Moon, which seems to emanate from it.","The silvery colour of the light reflected by the Moon."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To do a moonlight flit: to secretly leave premises without paying the rent, supposedly at night by the light of the Moon.","To do a moonlight flit: to secretly leave premises without paying the rent, supposedly at night by the light of the Moon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moonlight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sylph","blocks":[{"text":"A sylph is an air spirit stemming from the 16th-century works of Paracelsus, who describes sylphs as (invisible) beings of the air, his elementals of air. A significant number of subsequent literary and occult works have been inspired by Paracelsus's concept: Robert Alfred Vaughan noted that \"the wild but poetical fantasies\" of Paracelsus had probably exercised a larger influence over his age and the subsequent one than is generally supposed, particularly on the Rosicrucians, but that through the 18th century they had become reduced to \"machinery for the playwright\" and \"opera figurantes with wings of gauze and spangles\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Praetorius%281666%29-p0000-11Luftleute%26Windmenschen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylph","wikidata":"Q853329"},{"title":"Moonlight","blocks":[{"text":"Moonlight is light from the surface of the Moon, consisting mostly of reflected sunlight, and some earthlight."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Earthshine_Moon.jpg/330px-Earthshine_Moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonlight","wikidata":"Q1058800"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/500px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/1280px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/500px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Swiss alchemist and philosopher Theophrastus von Hohenheim (\"Paracelsus\") first created the concept of the sylph.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParacelsus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/500px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/1280px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/500px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"La Sylphide Bourbon, A.M. Bininger & Co. Bourbon advertising label in the shape of a glass showing a man pursuing three sylphs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg"},{"s